const editorial = {
  heading: "Why the music died at your last party — and the cheap fix nobody explains",
  html: `
<p>Every host has a version of the same story. The food is out, the yard is full, somebody asks you to turn it up — and the speaker you brought starts to fall apart. The reviews for cheap party speakers tell it in the owners' own words: <em>"can't go above 50% volume, speaker starts to distort, and it now has a constant crackle regardless of volume."</em> <em>"They have zero depth, zero bass, and really aren't that loud."</em> <em>"Dies after like 1 1/2- 2hrs."</em></p>

<p>Here's the part almost nobody gets told: that speaker didn't fail because it was cheap. It failed for a specific, physical, fixable reason — and once you know it, the whole category sorts itself into keepers and junk in about thirty seconds.</p>

<h3>One driver, two jobs</h3>

<p>Open up a $30 party speaker and you find one small driver doing everything. Bass and treble, at the same time, at volume, outdoors. Bass is the killer: producing it takes big physical movement, and a small driver runs out of travel fast. Push past that point and the sound clips. That clipping is the crackle. Not wear. Not bad luck. One component doing two jobs.</p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-sound.jpg" alt="Walla Sound Boom+ with 360-degree sound coverage — clear, well-balanced output that holds up at higher volume" width="739" height="988" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="max-width:340px;display:block;margin:0 auto;" />
  <figcaption>Two drivers, two jobs: the woofer takes the bass, the tweeter takes the highs — and neither is ever pushed past what it can do.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The fix has been standard in serious audio for seventy years: split the work. A JBL owner reviewing his own speaker explained it better than any spec sheet: <em>"I fully attribute this to the added tweeter, which eases the burden of the main driver from producing all frequencies."</em> That's a customer, unprompted, describing the exact mechanism.</p>

<p>The Boom+ runs that split — a 15W woofer and a 5W tweeter, printed on the box — at a price where almost everything else is still one lonely driver doing two jobs. That, not an adjective, is why it holds together at the volume where the speaker in your garage gives up. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)">See the full spec on the #1 card</a>.</p>

<h3>The battery number on the box is not the battery number in your yard</h3>

<p>The second way a party dies is quieter: the speaker just stops. And here the whole category — famous names included — has a ratings problem. The JBL Charge 6 is rated at 24 hours; SoundGuys measured it at <strong>13 hours and 15 minutes</strong> at realistic volume. Owners are blunter: <em>"I do not know how they claim 15 hours. I get 3-4 hours outdoors with full volume."</em> <em>"This one? 2 hours and the red light is blinking."</em></p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-battery.jpg" alt="Walla Sound Boom+ battery: 4000mAh capacity, 8 hours playback, 2 hours charging over USB-C" width="800" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>The numbers as the maker states them: 4000mAh, 8 hours of playback, 2 hours to recharge over USB-C.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>We can't fix the industry's ratings. What we can do is refuse to play: every battery figure on this page is printed as a <em>rating</em>, attributed to its maker, and where an independent measurement exists we cite it — including when it counts against the brands we rank. The Boom+ is rated 8 hours off a 4000mAh cell, and refills in about 2 hours over USB-C — the same cable as your phone. Treat every number in this category the same way, and 8 rated hours covers a BBQ that starts at four and ends in the dark.</p>

<h3>So why not just buy the JBL?</h3>

<p>Let's take the strongest version of the objection, because you'll have it: a JBL Flip 7 discounts to about a hundred dollars, it's a real woofer-and-tweeter design too, and it's a genuinely excellent small speaker. We ranked it on this page and said so.</p>

<p>Here's what the badge doesn't change: physics. Outdoors there are no walls to reflect sound. Every doubling of distance from a single speaker costs you about six decibels — so one box, <em>any</em> one box, gives you a loud circle of chairs and a quiet everywhere-else. The people at the far table aren't hearing your speaker, whatever logo is on the grille.</p>

<p>The fix isn't a louder box. It's two boxes, spread apart, playing true left and right. And this is where the price sheet stops being boring: <strong>a Boom+ stereo pair is $179.98. A Flip 7 pair lists at $259.90. One Charge 6 lists at $199.95 — and it's still mono.</strong></p>

<p>That's the entire argument of this page in three numbers. The market's own words for how a challenger brand earns the money: <em>"Best $70 speaker you can get imo. Competes with the jbl extreme3."</em> Nobody talks a friend into a spec sheet. They talk them into a comparison — and this one you can check from your phone in ninety seconds. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>Check today's 2-pack price →</strong></a></p>

<h3>What we'd skip, and who should buy something else</h3>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-water.jpg" alt="Walla Sound Boom+ on a rock in front of a waterfall — IP67 rated, all-weather ready" width="742" height="988" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>IP67: dust-tight, and rated for a metre of water for half an hour. The rating is printed on the box.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>If one speaker has to carry a genuinely big, loud space on its own — a tailgate, a block party — buy wattage: that's rank 3's job, and it does it at rank 3's size and price. If you just want something for the kitchen bench, the cheap option at rank 5 is honestly enough.</p>

<p>Everyone else — the birthday in the backyard, the pool afternoon, the deck at dusk — is who this ranking is for.</p>

<h3>Buy one, or buy two</h3>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-pair.jpg" alt="Walla Sound Boom+ surviving a splash, and the four available colors side by side" width="791" height="420" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>Four colors, one pairing button. Two units twenty feet apart, and there is no quiet end of the yard.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>One Boom+ covers the patio. The reason to look hard at the 2-pack — the maker's own "Most Popular" option — is what happens at the second press of a button: the units pair into true stereo, a left and a right, not the same mono signal twice, and the price drops to $89.99 each. An owner, after doing exactly this: <em>"I love it so much I purchased a second one to pair with the first one I bought and it was even better."</em> The 3-pack drops it again, to $79.99 a speaker.</p>

<p>Ninety days of full-refund-or-replacement applies whichever you pick, and shipping is free. That window exists because the maker knows exactly what an unknown brand has to prove — and it's triple what the store gives you. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>See single and 2-pack pricing →</strong></a></p>

<h3>The next party is already on the calendar</h3>

<p>You know the moment this page exists to delete: the food out, the yard full, the volume up — and that first electrical crackle in front of everyone. The speaker that did that to you will do it again. It is built to. Nothing about it changes between now and your next party except how much warning you had.</p>

<p>Here is the whole decision, priced out. Fixing it costs $99.99 — half off, shipped free, and completely reversible for 90 days. If it doesn't fill your yard, send it back and pay nothing. Keeping the old speaker costs nothing today and one more silence in front of your guests later. You already know which one of those you'll actually regret.</p>

<p><strong>Four of these five ask you to pay for a single box and hope it reaches the back fence. One is priced so you can stop hoping — and put a speaker at both ends of the yard.</strong></p>

<p><a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>Get the Boom+ — 50% off with free shipping →</strong></a></p>
`,
};

